// Type checking without a build step (ROADMAP.md 1.4): `npm run typecheck`,
// in CI. The JavaScript stays JavaScript; TypeScript only reads it, with the
// JSDoc already there as the types. It starts with what `npm run
// bundle:scene` ships to studio-api — src/scene-ops.js and every module it
// imports — because a mistake there breaks the platform as well as the app.
// That build runs in a Cloudflare Worker, so the globals are a worker's
// ("webworker"), not the page's, and "types" keeps every @types package out
// (eslint brings some): a `document`, `window` or `localStorage` anywhere in
// those modules fails here, before it can fail in studio-api. (That is why
// the canvas half of the image edits is src/image-paint.js.) A known gap: a
// browser's worker has a few things a Cloudflare Worker doesn't (indexedDB,
// OffscreenCanvas, createImageBitmap, XMLHttpRequest), and those would pass.
// @cloudflare/workers-types would close it, but it declares `crypto` as a
// const, which TypeScript leaves off `globalThis`, and model.js, placement.js
// and row.js read `globalThis.crypto` — a change to them is a change to the
// bundle studio-api vendors, so it waits for a pull request that pairs.
// Not strict: the aim is to catch a wrong call, not to annotate every line.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "target": "es2022",
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["es2022", "webworker"],
    "types": [],
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "files": ["src/scene-ops.js"]
}
